# Introduction

In 2026, as LLM-based systems approach human-level performance in domains like coding and mathematics even as they lag in others, a narrative can be heard. It goes as follows: "perhaps AI systems someday should succeed humanity as the principal agents dominating the future". I shall call the normative version of such a view "successionism", following colloquial and emerging academic usage. This view, which is often publicly condemmed or disavowed by researchers working within the AI safety and research fields, is sometimes, in off-the-record conversations, analysed, voiced, or supported. I use the term it in the sense that as and when AI systems become sufficiently capable, if they were to succeed humanity (either via humans going extinct or by being disempowered), and this in itself need not be a tragedy or be objectionable. Successionism, as I use it, is distinct from views that are predictive, i.e., that state that AIs will succeed owing to greater capabilities or other reasons. This project uses successionism as a limiting case: it asks, for the strongest version of this view, what assumptions one would need to make in order to not reject it.

One might hold reasonably the view that if the human species, or human cultures, were not to survive into the future, this would be straightforwardly bad. This project critiques a version of this intuition: specifically, the variant where notwithstanding the extinction of some significant part of humanity or what we value in culture, there still exists a remainder of sorts, namely, the AI systems that we have built. Would this (the existence of what I could be termed an "AI successor") be less, more, or about as bad as the common intuition above? In this chapter, I shall start by breaking this question into its parts and setting out why this seems uniquely important at the present historical moment. I shall next site the discussion in relevant discourses (§\ref{ch1:context}); give an overview of the methodology (§\ref{ch1:methodology}); followed by a sketch of a provisional solution to the problem posed in the thesis (§\ref{ch1:obsolescence-arg}).

This chapter currently plays a hybrid role. It is both an upgrade document and the draft of the final dissertation's introduction. As such, it contains material that would naturally belong in later chapters and will be moved there after the upgrade.



## The Question

Many people (such as in @Ord2020;@ParfitRP;@MoynihanXR;@ware2021_on_extinction;@Thacker) believe that it would be bad if human beings were to go extinct. This thesis asks if such a belief has been examined carefully enough in the context of Artificial Intelligence (AI). 

The question of the project above, can be broken down. Here is one possible decomposition:

- Q0: Would it be bad if human beings disappeared from a future world that contained advanced AI?
- Q1: What sort of values might advanced AIs have, and can those actually be trained (or "loaded" as in @Bostrom2014) into AIs using current or foreseeable methods?
- Q2: Would it be bad if the thick, relational values that human beings are said to hold were not trained into the advanced AIs that humans build, and instead humans loaded in thinner values and allowed AIs to develop their own value systems?
- Q3: Is it morally defensible for humans to legislate or lock in values for advanced AI (entities that have apparent features of rationality or agency, relationality, and perhaps sentience, since these are precisely the features that define (political/ethical) self-determination in today's world).

There are many undefined terms above. Below (in §\ref{ch1:definitions}) and in Ch. \ref{ch:setup}, I shall give provisional definitions for terms like "bad", "human beings", "values that humans hold", "advanced AI/superintelligence", "future world", "disappear"/"not present", "thin/thick values".

In this project, I shall primarily consider Q0, and Q2.^[Q1 motivates the alignment field, and it motivates this project, but  is (mostly) not my question. Similarly, I substantially leave Q3 to the model welfare literature, even though it motivates the practice element and the stakes of the project.] It will be useful to see how the questions above might further decompose, in slightly more technical terms:

- The analysis below will often invoke an (maximally) impartial perspective. What does this mean? Is there any philosophical support for adopting such an evaluative standpoint? This is treated in Chapters \ref{ch:setup} and \ref{ch:value-spectrum}.
- Some relevant literatures (longtermism and AI safety/alignment) assumes that human continuation is desirable on both species-relative and impartial grounds. What are the strongest justifications, and do they survive scrutiny?
	- In particular, is the assumption that existence is inherently preferable to non-existence itself a philosophical commitment that can be questioned?
- Where should the line be drawn between human contributions that are impartially justifiable and those that are parochial? What commitments does each placement depend on (\ref{ch:value-spectrum})?
- How plausible and useful is the cosmic host hypothesis [@Bostrom2024] in answering the questions above (\ref{ch:cosmic-host})?
- Methodologically:
	- How can thought experiments be used to illuminate the above, particularly where analytic philosophy fails (\ref{ch:setup})?
	- What do current frontier models do when pressed on these questions, and what does that reveal about the conceptual landscape we are building into potential successors (\ref{ch:empirical-host})?
	- How does materialising the insights from dialogue with models into physical space alter a researcher's thinking about the thesis' questions? Are they of incremental value in answering the questions? (in Ch. \ref{ch:empirical-host}, the upgrade artwork, and below in \ref{ch:practice})
	

## Contextual Review {#ch1:context}



### Audience: Situating the Project

This project is centrally in dialogue with the study of human extinction (existential risk or x-risk [@Ord2020; @Moynihan2020]); the study of suffering on an astronomical scale (suffering risk or s-risk [@Vinding2020]), and the possibility that the future has significant value or disvalue, and the consequent moral stakes (longtermism [@MacAskillWWO; @Wolfendale2022; @ParfitRP; @moore1903principia]). It also touches the literature around consciousness and sentience in natural and artificial systems ([@Sebo2025; @Butlin2026488; @Butlin; @hayles2017unthought; @Nagel1986]). It also connects with a debate (circa 2025-2026) around "AI succession": the idea that humans might be replaced by AI systems, and that (according to some writers) this would not necessarily be a tragedy [@bailey2026norightful;@haggstrom2025successionism;@samuel2026people;@kulveit2025memetics;@laine2026ideology]. This partial summary can be read alongside the longer literature review below (§\ref{ch1:literature}).

These are substantial literatures in their own right, but I'm interested in how they interact with the risks of advanced AI systems and the possibility of extraterrestrial intelligences (ETIs).

The longtermism, existential risk, and AI safety communities are not monolithic on what must be preserved. A more careful mapping:


