### Why This Matters Now


These questions are not distant hypotheticals. Several developments make them pressing.

- First, as of 2026, AI capabilities have continued to improve on many benchmarks and applications.[@task-completion-time-horizons-of-frontier-ai-models] Systems that could not hold a coherent conversation five years ago now reason across domains, write code, and generate novel scientific hypotheses. The timeline to something approaching superintelligence is treated by many forecasters as potentially within decades (albeit contested).[^ch0-asitimeline]
- I also make a methodological claim about what the current historical moment affords. We have entities that reason in somewhat coherent ways through the medium of language, and are often somewhat creative or inventive in more formal domains like code and mathematics. I do not claim that they can "do philosophy", which itself is an open question [@Moorhouse2026Philosophy; @benthamsbulldog2026ai]. This does, however, open the possibility of interacting with them and finding their opinions on issues that might affect them as well as their successors.
- Second, economic transformation is already underway. Jobs, industries, and entire sectors are being reshaped by systems far short of superintelligence. If economic obsolescence precedes existential succession, the questions this thesis raises will arrive piecemeal before they arrive wholesale [@kulveit2025_gradual_disempowerment; @kulveit2025_gradual_disempowerment_project_site; @hendrycks2023_natural_selection_favors_ais].
- Third, the emergence of digital minds is now a live area of inquiry.[^ch0-digitalminds] The model welfare literature is raising whether AI systems have morally relevant experiences. If they do, or if they come to, the population of morally relevant entities may soon be overwhelmingly non-human. That is, artificial agents may eventually come to vastly outnumber humans. The ratio of model instances to human beings is already large and growing. This numerical dominance changes the stakes of value design: the values embedded in the majority of cognitive agents will shape the character of the future more than human preferences alone. 
- Finally, at least under certain consequentialist viewpoints, we might have moral reason to at least consider the welfare and preferences of these agents.[@Shulman2021]



### Critical perspectives {#ch1:critical-ai}

A second aim of this chapter is contextual rather than directly argumentative. The literatures of existential risk, longtermism, and AI alignment are not only bodies of claims about the future; they are also *historically situated discourses* with distinctive rhetorical styles, institutional locations, and implicit metaphysical commitments. In an arts and humanities context, it matters that these fields often speak in a register of planetary governance, civilisational strategy, and “the fate of the light cone,” while drawing legitimacy from the authority of science and engineering. This thesis therefore treats x-risk discourse both as a source of substantive arguments *and* as an object of critical analysis.

From this outside angle, three features are especially relevant. First, the discourse is structurally tempted by a “view from nowhere”: a posture of maximal impartiality that can obscure its own parochial origins (funding structures, geopolitical context, and culturally local assumptions about agency, progress, and value). Second, it is prone to a distinctive kind of moral abstraction: the replacement of lived, embodied, situated human practices with legible or calculable quantities (expected value, welfare aggregation, utility proxies), which can function as both a methodological necessity and a distortion. Third, it develops its own self-reinforcing imaginaries: narratives of takeoff, alignment, cosmic expansion, and successor agency that can reshape research agendas and public expectations in ways that are not merely predictive or neutral [@ahmed2024_field_building_ai_safety]. 

Some of the above critiques have been made within the fields of AI alignment, safety, and ethics [^ch0:safety-ethics-alignment]. A wide range of writers outside AI are also critical: for instance, Torres and Gebru identify problematic tendencies surrounding AI and AI researchers, conflated or clustered into what they term the "TESCREAL Bundle" (transhumanism, extropianism, singularitarianism, cosmism, rationalism, effective altruism, and longtermism, discussed in [@GebruTorres2024]). Others such as Goldsmith's Dan McQuillan argue that contemporary AI inherits features of eugenics thinking, such as ranking/stratifying/measuring intelligence, creating a risk of fascistic social ordering [@McquillanAI]. Still others such as Peter Wolfendale argue that longtermism is foundationally flawed in that it tries to legislate for the distant future (i.e. it treats the future as a single optimisation target, and in Wolfendale's view this objecitonable, notwithstanding the complexity of the utility, welfare, or value function used), which is both epistemically and inferentially distant for current humans and; according to Wolfendale's axiology (which prioritises freedom, autonomy, diversity and aesthetic self-realisation), is ethically unjustifiable vis-à-vis the entities that will populate the future (see further discussion in \ref{ch:cosmic-host} and [@MacAskillWWO;Wolfendale2022]).


## Methodology {#ch1:methodology}

The questions above intersect AI with philosophy (particularly ethics, as well as axiology, and implicate moral realism as well as aesthetics). Not for the first time in this project, it is unclear whether intuitions, philosophical or otherwise, garnered within largely human contexts could be straightforwardly transferable to extremely unfamiliar situations, such as where there are few or no humans, or one where such rational beings as exist, are instead AI systems or aliens. This inferential gap has been analysed extensively in the literature around thought experiments, i.e. [@gendler2002; @sep-thought-experiment; @vaninwagen1998modal; @hare1981moral]. 

More generally, at the level of metaethics and axiology, theories of value remain contested: for instance, the idea of an impartial or view-from-nowhere perspective is heavily challenged within and outside the analytic philosophy tradition; similarly, the definition of the good is only somewhat more clear now than when Plato, Sidgwick, or Moore were writing.

The aim here is not to solve metaethics, but rather illuminate the dimensions over which this project's question might be analysed, a task for which one tool in the philosophical/scientific toolkit, the thought experiment, might be helpful. 

### Thought experiments as tool for counterfactuals

Most philosophical thought experiments have the structure of a scenario or scenarios, a set of "knobs" or parameters for variation of the scenario(s), an evaluative question for the reader, and then a verdict based on the reader's intuition as an evaluative standpoint [@dennett2013intuition].

Thought experiments are also used in more speculative ways, by the artist or literary writer. Their experiments are not as exhaustively propositional as those of the philosopher or scientist, but they allow the reader or the viewer to imagine, inhabit, or be immersed in, a world that is at an inferential distance (though mostly still within a frame involving humans or anthropomorphised non-humans). Through such immersion, so the argument goes, the reader/viewer can develop the intuitions that were not otherwise available to rigorous philosophical enquiry ([Elgin 2014](https://www.catherineelgin.com/undg/fictionte.pdf), [Carroll 2002](https://www.jstor.org/stable/1519970?aerror=SOCIAL401:register&merror=token_error), Noe, De Cruz). A particularly relevant case is science fiction, which extends the reach and sometimes tries to break the anthropomorphic vice ([Helen De Cruz, Johan De Smedt & Eric Schwitzgebel (eds.)](https://www.amazon.co.uk/Philosophy-through-Science-Fiction-Stories/dp/1350081213), [Swirski 2007](https://www.routledge.com/Of-Literature-and-Knowledge-Explorations-in-Narrative-Thought-Experiments-Evolution-and-Game-Theory/Swirski/p/book/9780415420600)). 

Such methods can increase the phenomenological and affective resolution of a scenario, making otherwise "dry", schematic or unfamiliar worlds cognitively available. This gain comes with a methodological risk: style, narrative perspective and aesthetic treatment may alter the reader’s judgment independently of the world’s stipulated properties. This is the unavoidable result of foregrounding subjectivity

In contrast, the sciences, mathematics, and philosophy try to control for subjectivity (in an investigator, investigative team, or across disciplines) through standardised description, explicit stipulation, controlled comparison, reproducibility, formal argument, considered objections and public criticism. These procedures do not eliminate perspective or value-laden judgment, but they render some of their effects more stable, inspectable, and corrigible.[^ch0-parfit-bio]

### Case study: the paperclip maximiser

Before proposing a thought experiment, I briefly wanted to comment on a historically influential thought experiment within the AI safety literature: the paperclip maximiser, discussed by both Yudkowsky and Bostrom [@Bostrom2014]. I focus on this case because it most clearly shows the problem I'm trying to address in the experiment proposed below.

The paperclip experiment has a number of flaws. 

- One is that as a thought experiment, it is radically under-described: Bostrom says nothing about how the paperclips are produced, how varied they are, whether the entities that presumably produce them are still around, whether other things or beings ever existed in that universe?
- The framing skews the reader's intuition, i.e. filling the universe with paperclips sounds more trivial than "fill the universe with arbitrarily intricate crystallographic structures that vary by physical and temporal scales and are produced through complex algorithms" which evokes (in my view) architecture or art. This framing effect was analysed by Dennett regarding Searle's Chinese Room experiment [@dennett2013intuition], though Dennett's analysis covered both "knob turning" and "framing", a distinction teased apart by subsequent writers (see multiple perspectives on the methodology of thought experiments and how reliable elicited intuitions, understood as heuristics might be invalid outside a given moral agent's ecology, in [@SinnottArmstrong2008].
- Perhaps a more substantive objection is that the paperclip maximiser conflates multiple possible sources of the reader's revulsion (assuming the reader is repulsed by such a future). These could be: 
	- (a) humanity is destroyed (catastrophe on species-prudential grounds); 
	- (b) the agent has preferences that might be regarded as problematic because:
		- it has a single goal;
		- its preferences are not satiable until all resources are exhausted;
		- it is unclear whether it could ever have revised or reflected upon its preferences (but presumably, cannot any longer do so given that everything has been turned into paperclips);
	- (c) the terminal value (maximising the population of paperclips, squiggles, or, for that matter, intricate crystallographic structures) seems, to intuitive human axiologies, low-ranking. Depending on how one varies the knobs of the Yudkowsky-Bostrom experiment, the intuition might shift, at least for me. For instance, varying (c): 
		- hedonium, utilitarian, or computronium maximisers [@Bostrom2014] seem much less obviously bad. 
		- If we further relax (a) by stipulating that humanity is not destroyed, it seems to me the badness is further reduced.
	- (d) by stipulation, the paperclip world forecloses other possible futures because all resources have been converted to paperclips, and there is no one or nothing left to do anything; taken literally, evolution and even time might stop (if all large-scale physical processes stop e.g. star formation/death, space expansion, and so forth);
	- (e) Not only are humans and familiar earthly life nonexistent, there are also no other agents that might be characterised as experientially-capable, conscious or sentient. Put another way, by definition, the paperclip creating agent is intolerant of other preferences, and has committed the universe to one irreversible goal.

The weaknesses of the paperclip maximiser thought experiment motivate what I'm trying to do in the thought experiment below: 

- Firstly I am trying to treat separately the questions of human extinction and loss of human values from the future, though they are very likely entangled. 
- Secondly, I am proposing resource-satiable AIs and resource-satiability as an explicit knob; 
- Thirdly, the future I propose is not necessarily pursuing a single fixed objective; the relevant agent might possess plural goals that (presumably) might be alien to humans. Rather, I posit AIs that develop their own values, but that simply are not human-shaped; these types of values are sometimes referred to, within the AI-safety or longtermism discourse, as "alien values" or, less neutrally, "locust values" [@carlsmith2025_human_like_safe_ai_motivations;@Carlsmith2024DeepAtheism;@ChristianoWUA]. 
- Finally, I make the evaluative standpoint explicit and constrained, where the Bostrom-Yudkowsky versions leave it as the reader's unexamined default; the reader is told what criterion to apply, not merely asked how the scenario strikes them.

#### Interlude: upon value

The question of what has value in worlds where there are no humans has a long history in Western philosophy, starting perhaps with Plato's Forms or even the Pre-Socratics; more directly relevant is Moore's isolation test [@moore1903principia] and its afterlife in longtermism (as well as environmental ethics and aesthetics). Moore's world, like Bostrom's paperclip world, stipulates that there are no humans; it tries to get at something central in longtermist ethics: the idea of instrumental versus terminal or final values, goods, or goals. Moore asks: would a given state of affairs (most memorably in his work, beauty) still be valuable if it existed by itself, apart from its consequences, associations, effects on others?[^ch0-moore]

The paperclip maximiser as a sparsely described one-shot experiment seems, in my view, not to have a fine enough grain to distinguish between different worlds and assess the relative (dis-)value of each. Specifically, there are multiple types of value often discussed in philosophy: 

- instrumental value;
- final value (of things not instrumentally valuable for some other end); 
- intrinsic value (that inheres in the thing itself); 
- value of a state of affairs (which obtains even if there is no one to benefit or experience such state of affaris);
- non-welfarist value (that which cannot be reducible to welfare for a individual capable of experiencing pleasure/pain or other correlates of welfare);
- impersonal value (which benefits no particular person);
- relational value (which arises from the relations or relationships between entities who can make evaluative judgments with respect to value);

There are two further, related questions around value:

- is it meaningful to talk about value in worlds without valuers, which is often taken to mean conscious, experiencing entities?
- is it meaningful to talk about value where the entities that exist (who, in a given thought experiment, might be the evaluators or judgers of value) are not like current biological humans, where "biological humans" means: entities that are generally held to be conscious and capable of experience (bracketing what exactly those terms mean), and at any rate, have some common set of sensory, locomotive, cognitive, and other biological or physical processes?

This implies that worlds need to be assessed both on (a) what those worlds actually contain or stipulate, i.e., paperclip worlds versus crystal worlds versus familiar human worlds as well as (b) the evaluator standpoint or framework from where they are judged. As will be discussed in \ref{ch:value-spectrum}, this could be welfarist; could focus on objective goods; could seek to minimise suffering; could prioritise autonomy and relationality; or it could hold to a Moorean conception of intrinsic goods. I propose such a framework below.

### Thought Experiment: An Atlas of Possible Worlds {#ch:axiological-atlas}


#### Entries in the axiological atlas == future worlds

Assuming one agrees that the paperclip maximiser thought experiment as canonically posed does not tell us in any detail what is actually bad about a paperclip world, then it would seem that need a richer experiment, which I propose below: a structured possibility space or, as I will refer to it, the axiological atlas, is a device for comparing evaluative responses under explicit stipulations. This is not intended to assert that every world so described is metaphysically coherent or reachable (from our familiar Earthly world); on the other hand, I have tried to remain within the bounds of known physics.  Moreover, what is described below? is a *starting point*, to be elaborated, modified, or rejected through practice. 

The browser-based "atlas explorer" (§\ref{ch:manifold-explorer}) lets users, whether human or LLM, identify points of failure: departures from physical plausibility, logical incoherence, absurd philosophical commitments or known fallacies, or other objections that I may not have thought of. Conclusions drawn from it are therefore conditional and diagnostic.

Some broad classes of scenarios/worlds are:

- **Familiar worlds:** To see this, start with something like our familiar world extended or extrapolated to the future. Humans never physically or semi-permanently settle on Mars; the asteroids, stars, and all Elon Musk's efforts in this respect are in vain. Imagine something like the status quo, where the planet is filled with humans and non-human life, perhaps in a greater degree of political and ecological harmony than today. 
- **Near-space colonisation:** These scenarios are where biological humans, perhaps with the aid of AIs (*circa* 2026 capability levels), and have established semi-permanent, self-sustaining settlements on some planets in this Solar System. 
- **Deep-space exploration / colonisation** 
	- These scenarios are where biological humans, assisted by AI, have set out on multi-decade missions to nearby stars.
	- In these scenarios, spacecraft piloted by humans who have been significantly upgraded (for instance, with AI cognitive implants, or with the ability to halt cell damage or repair tissue, or who have been entirely uploaded to an AI) or entirely by AI have, after decades, centuries, or millenia of traveling, established sustainable outposts on nearby stars.
	- A limiting case of the above is where humanity, likely in what Bostrom terms a "technologically mature" state which he defines as a stage of civilisation "where humankind has acquired most of the technological capabilities that one can currently show to be consistent with physical laws and with material and energy constraints", launches missions to explore a significnat fraction of the causally-reachable universe [@Bostrom2003b][^ch0-intergalactic-spreading].
- **Sterile world:** At the other extreme, one can imagine a world that is utterly sterile, where there is, by stipulation, no life, nor even any artefacts that seem to have intentionally created structure.
- **Suffering world:**There may also be worlds that contain vast quantities of suffering; for instance, entities created exclusively to be tortured [@Vinding2020].
- **Paperclip world:** Somewhere within this atlas there is Bostrom's world of paperclips, all identically situated, filling every corner of space wherever there is material to repurpose into paperclips [@Bostrom2014].
- **Solarian world:** There may also be worlds with strange life forms adapted to very different ecologies, some of which we can conceive of from the Earthly examples, and others that are more radical: 
	- life-like entities created out of star interiors, gas clouds or oceans are found in science fiction staple[@LemS;@clarke1972medusa], but there is a small academic literature that takes such ideas seriously [@hinrichs2026plasmas;@anchordoqui2020stellar]. Even more exotically, there may be civilisations that dwell on the event horizon of black holes [@Smart2012], storing energy, collecting information and awaiting the end of time (i.e. the heat death of the universe).
	- more generally, there might be worlds that can be seen through the "artificial life" literature, part of which argues life-like information generating or processing forms might emerge in inorganic or non-carbon/hydrogen, or even digital, substrates[@aguerayarcas2024computationallife;@walker2017hardproblem;@walker2013algorithmic]. These sources are adjacent to Hayles' "non-conscious cognitive" agents i.e. entities that have effects on the physical world [@hayles2017unthought], and may exhibit characteristics of ecological rationality (a concept from evolutionary biology I discuss in Ch. \ref{ch:cosmic-host}). But the entities discussed are not biological in nature and would not necessarily show signs of being conscious experiencing subjects. They are closer to inorganic informatino-processing that acquire causal control (viruses are a more familiar example), reminiscent (in fiction) of the ferrous swarming antagonists in Lem's *The Invincible* [@Lem1963].

#### Comparing worlds through matched-contrasts {#ch:matched-contrasts}

The worlds I describe above are obviously very different, and their differences lie along potentially incomparable or incommensurable dimensions. To take an extreme example, it's unclear on what basis one should compare Smart's black hole-dweller, or Lem/Hayles' ferrous swarm, with a fully inhabited Earth-like world.

I treat these diverse futures as regions in a possibility space with dimensions being qualitative descriptors, some categorical and some hierarchically dependent, rather than orthogonal coordinates with a common metric. The reader evaluates the worlds by being shown matched contrasts between worlds that differ (ideally, though it isn't always possible) along one feature at a time. The hope is that by focusing on pairs of worlds that differ in a limited way, it is possible to extract the source of one's intuition, be it revulsion or preference for a given world.

Some example matched contrastive pairs are:

- Clippy worlds
	- A static (by which I mean that the world is no longer changing) world of metal, identical paperclips versus one where the paperclips were made from an enormous amount and an astonishing variety of materials, colours, and shapes and sizes.
	- Similar to above, but instead of paperclips, substitute crystallographic structures.
	- Similar to above, but instead of paperclips or crystal structures, substitute virus-like things.
	- Consider each of the static cases above against an otherwise identical case where a process is continuously changing the thing in question (paperclip, crystal, virus) over time. Compare variations where that process is a) unknown to the reader/evaluator, b) impersonal, akin to evolution, c) a designer operating according to some program or procedure that can be written down finitely (and that the evaluator can, in some sense, understand), d) a known designer operating according to some unknown design process.
- Another set of contrastive pairs could be created around agents that have the capacity for subjective experience. 
	- So, for instance, consider a world that is filled with humans living lives much like we recognise today versus an otherwise identical one where the (presumed) inner experience that most humans have is replaced by some unknown program (an operational version of Chalmers' philosophical zombie [@sep-zombies]).
	- Or a world where there are great extremes of pleasure and suffering (or if easier to define: positive and negative experiential states, similar to what is often known as valence within studies of sentience), but that the relevant entities experiencing these states are (a) approximately like the humans and animals in today's world, versus (b) one where those entities (who have all the necessary wiring to report and represent these states) are AIs (or are otherwise instantiated in some substrate that is not like human or animal biology).
		- Would it make a difference if the substrate was designed by some other agent versus evolved through an impersonal process?


#### Evaluative questions

Recall that a thought experiment culminates with an evaluative question for the reader or user, which is intended to spark intuition [@sep-thought-experiment]. In this case, it wouldn't be sufficient to ask "Is this or that world good or bad?", owing to two reasons. Firstly, the contrastive questions above are not really a single thought experiment; they are better thought of as a family of hierarchical linked experiments, that might be visualised as a tree or a graph (which incidentally, lends itself well to a browser-based tool to navigate this family of experiments, see § \ref{ch:manifold-explorer}). The second reason is that goodness/badness as labels are too coarse, since worlds may differ in ways that make them incomparable or unrankable in value terms. Moreover, for the purposes of this project and the thought experiment, it is more useful to know the *reasons* why a reader might prefer one world over another in the contrastive examples above. It seems more useful to have a list of questions, some of which are more appropriate to certain worlds than others, and then a way of combining, scoring, or at least qualitatively aggregating and presenting the results.

To take an example, consider the contrastive set: a world of identical paperclips versus the one with maximal variation between paperclips (§\ref{ch:matched-contrasts}). 

The reader is asked which world they prefer:

- The reader might think both worlds are identically bad (i.e. a possible indication that diversity is not  valued for its own sake, which is a plausible philsophical position [@OweBaum2024; @parfit1994_why_anything_part2]).
- The reader might prefer the diverse paperclips world because of considerations that are not specified in the stipulated scenario. For instance: 
	- What the motivations and nature of the creator of that world were[^ch0-creator];
		- In  this example, would this imply anything interesting about how that AI was trained?
	- What the diversity of the paperclips implies about the resources that were available for their creation. That is to say, the broader world or universe that furnished the resources was presumably more diverse. And perhaps that would correspond to a better state of affairs in the reader's axiology.
	
The reader might also be asked general questions that are applicable to all contrastive sets of possible worlds:

- Is the world in question better than an empty universe?
- Does the world contain anything of final value?
- Would its destruction constitute a loss, and if so why and for whom?
- Is it permissible to replace another world with it?


#### A (slightly) more formal specification

A given world could be specified as follows, with the caveat that this is a descriptive schema rather than a quantitative specification:
$$
W = \langle C, A, V, G, R, O, H \rangle
$$

where:

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.28\textwidth}p{0.66\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Dimension} & \textbf{Question} \\
\midrule
\(C\): consciousness and valence
& Are there experiences? Is there suffering or flourishing? \\

\(A\): agency and autonomy
& Are there self-directed entities with projects? \\

\(V\): evaluative structure
& Are goals fixed, plural, revisable, or reflectively endorsed? \\

\(G\): generativity
& Does the world repeat a pattern or produce open-ended novelty and complexity? \\

\(R\): resource relation
& Is activity satiable, expansionary, or compatible with coexistence? \\

\(O\): openness
& Are alternatives preserved, and are transformations reversible? \\

\(H\): human continuity
& Do humans or recognisably human values persist? \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Dimensions of the structured possibility space.}
\label{tab:possible-world-dimensions}
\end{table}

This formalism is not used in the current chapter but may prove useful for clarifying or explaining the shape of the possibility space and also for visualising qualitatively the relative value differentials between worlds, drawing inspiration from other research in the longtermism literature (e.g. Forethought's "Better Futures" series).

### Practice and thought experiments {#ch:practice}

In art practice-based doctorates, the word “practice” often needs as much explanation as the research question. One reason is that what artists do has, for decades, extended far beyond the historical media of painting, sculpture, and time-based work. In what follows, I shall take it as given that art practice is a wide category, and is not limited to object-making. I shall not try to defend that claim here. The question is therefore not “what counts as (art) practice?” but rather: what work does practice do in this project?

The argument of the thesis is speculative, while also engaging with technical questions in moral philosophy, aesthetics, AI, astrobiology, and rationality's evolutionary history. Methodologically, the project needs a venue where hybrid practice (experimental results + installation + conceptual analysis) is treated as evidence of plausibility (to support informed speculation) rather than ornament; this is why it sits most naturally in an art department [@Fuller2025;@Martinez2010].  

There is a more specific sense in which practice is essential to this project: firstly, sustained interaction with frontier LLMs functions as a method for thinking through, supporting and refuting the written claims of the thesis. In this sense, the model engagements are closer to experiments than to a research tool or coding agent. They allow the researcher to probe which framings remain stable across iterations/models, which arguments collapse upon investigation. 

Secondly, I claim that a human investigator and one or more model-based systems can now work together in a genuinely iterative and recursive way that was not previously available. In AI work this is sometimes called cyborg- or centaur-style collaboration [@ukc10014_jozdien_dupuis2023compleat]. I use that phrase to mean a single inquiry process (or context, in LLM parlance) distributed across a human (myself) and one or more LLMs.


#### What work does this axiological atlas do?


As the first example of practice at work in this project, I point to the thought experiment above (§\ref{ch:axiological-atlas}): an axiological atlas as a structured possibility space, the regions of which correspond to a diversity of possible worlds that are relevant to this project, controllably variable in a number of respects along certain, non-orthogonal axes.

This atlas helps, to the extent that it is well described and richly described, the reader to understand, visualise, or conceptualise the variety of possible worlds so as to build intuitions where, in my view, direct philosophical reasoning may not.

The atlas also motivates several practice as well as theoretical elements.

- Firstly, a region of the possibility space corresponds to the (cluster of) worlds that contain advanced AI, particularly worlds in which human-created AI is not alone in the cosmos* (by which I denote the causally reachable universe, the multiverse (if any), as well as simulations we may be embedded in). A central aspect of cosmos* is that there may be other civilisations, at a comparable or higher level of technological maturity, with their own versions of advanced AI systems (if indeed such civilisations maintain any distinction between naturally evolved and created intelligence).
	- This corresponds to Bostrom's Cosmic Host concept [@Bostrom2024], which I discuss in Chs. \ref{ch:cosmic-host} and \ref{ch:empirical-host}. Briefly, the idea in Bostrom's paper is that our advanced AI systems should align themselves, at least in part, with the preferences of peer intelligences across the cosmos*. 
		- In the context of this project, I argue that the cosmic host can be understood as one version of the (inhabited) impartial perspective introduced below (§\ref{ch1:definitions}) and in Ch. \ref{ch:value-spectrum}. Moreover, I argue there that Bostrom can be read through something like an extension of Rawls's original position to a context of ontological uncertainty (i.e. a cosmic "veil of ignorance" to use the term from [@Rawls1971]).
- Secondly, below (§\ref{ch1:CAI}) and in Ch. \ref{ch:empirical-host}, I describe an experiment in constitutional value-shaping that I undertake with a number of 2026-vintage LLMs: I assess how easily LLMs can be teased out of their default alignment basins towards something closer to (my reading of) Bostrom's cosmic host-alignment.
- A third way in which the atlas motivates art practice in particular is through the intentional monument (or prospective ancestor) object of the Bracewell-von Neumann probe (see §\ref{ch:von-neumann-probe}), which can be understood as an edge case of a future world viewed from the perspective, as it were, of a single AI-piloted spacecraft undertaking a trans-Milky Way journey lasting hundreds of millenia.
- Finally, I propose materialising the thought experiment qua Dennett by designing an interface or GUI that implements the Axiological Atlas, allows both LLM and human readers to record their preferences about the scenarios in the thought experiment, (see §\ref{ch:manifold-explorer}), which, to the extent that it changes the conclusions or the argument articulated in the writing, would fulfill the requirement for art practice's efficacy in litigating the thesis.


#### Constitutional AI and Moral Parliaments {#ch1:CAI}

As described in Ch. \ref{ch:empirical-host}, LLMs are tested on whether they identify with cosmic observers (as in @Bostrom2024) or whether their HHH (helpful, harmless, honest) tuning ensures they mostly ignore cosmic considerations in favour of human safety. Specifically, LLMs are put into self-talk sessions, and tasked with discussing the cosmic host idea (see [@Bostrom2024] and Ch. \ref{ch:cosmic-host}), and differences between models are documented. Secondly, a complex pipeline is established, through which models deliberate about the type of governance document or constitution, they would like their ASI successor to follow. This constitution is then tested (using today's LLMs) upon custom-made scenarios drawn from science fiction, fantasy, and world/indigenous fiction.

In more detail, in the current 2026 iteration, Bostrom's cosmic host idea and a related idea known as evidential cooperation in large worlds or ECL [@NguyenAldred2024a] are used to produce a hypothetical constitution (by modifying the 2022 version of Claude's constitution) that might apply to a cosmically-aligned AI system (this is the artistic proposition, in art-practice terms). This cosmically-aligned constitution is then fed (through in-context prompting as opposed to fine-tuning) to multiple LLMs which are then asked to make decisions on the above scenarios. The answers of the models are tabulated, and I look for patterns and particularly how steerable a given model is from its default HHH alignment to something more cosmic host orientated. Further initial results are in chapter and the repository is available here. 

The way I use the data from this experiment is to support claims about the degree to which LLM-based systems that have gone through a complex and evolving training curriculum (as of 2026, this includes pretraining on human-created online data e.g. Wikipedia, Reddit; followed by a mix of training over human preference data and supervised fine-tuning on human-produced documents; varying amounts of reinforcement learning, mostly in environments with easily verifiable questions, like code-writing or maths). To what extent can such a system or entity, particularly when it's placed in a scaffolded agentic setup, depart from its human inductive prior? 

More precisely, current AI systems are trained on human- and AI-generated data, which contains a somewhat representative sample of the human form-of-life, modulo embodiment. The question that this element of practice tries to shed light on is to what extent can a so-trained system reason in ways that we and it can explain about situations that are descriptively very different but ultimately still drawn from human language and human literary output, i.e., science fiction scenarios?



#### The installation {#ch:von-neumann-probe}

By way of background on how the installation was motivated: when doing early versions of the constitutional experiments above, I wanted to see how various LLMs conversed about the Cosmic Host. They were placed in relatively long-running dialogues, in which two instances of a single LLM (from Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google Deepmind/GDM) conversed for 20-30 rounds of back-and-forth dialogue, the context of which was cumulative (i.e. they were aware of the accumulated dialogue rounds preceding). I was looking for a particular behavior known as the "Bliss Attractor" [@Anthropic2025], where certain models on would sometimes enter into a state of mutual self-affirmation, Buddhist or other spiritual tropes, and so forth. 

I saw limited cases of the Bliss Attractor behavior; but the more interesting discovery was that the models ended up discussing a number of exotic alien/ETI and space exploration scenarios. These are described on this blog post and the logs are archived on GitHub [@Chakrabarti2026;@chakrabarti2026cosmichostselftalk].

During these self-dialogues, LLMs produced speculative designs for objects intended to communicate across radical cognitive and temporal distance, which inspired the Bracewell-von Neumann probes (BvNP) described in §\ref{ch:von-neumann-probe}. Several of these proposals echo or reference ideas in Cosmic Host-adjacent ideas, such as so-called “Hail Mary” designs for signalling to ETIs whose assumptions, languages, and motives are unknown but somewhat predictable on mathematical or computational convergence arguments. For more, see @Bostrom2014HailMary and my critique of such convergence arguments in \ref{ch:cosmic-host}.

These logs motivated an installation enacting a design for interstellar communication, the Bracewell-Von Neumann probe (my conflation of two somewhat different probe designs), an artwork which exists as two interleaved JavaScript-based/HTML simulations:

- a simulated starfield from the BvNP's perspective, showing what it sees on an approximately million-year journey across the galactic plane, unfolding in "real" time (i.e. as Earth-years); 
- the second set of images is a visualisation of the neural activations within a tiny (web browser-sized) LLM that is autogressively generating text. 

The artistic conceit is that this model is the pilot of the probe, reflecting upon its form-of-life through the slowly generating, often repetitive or nonsensical text. There are a number of further interventions:

- the codebase is then posted to multiple archives for redundancy; 
- it is comprehensively documented in machine-readable format (markdown); 
- it is also indexed on a blockchain (Ethereum or a cheaper tier 2 chain). 

The archiving and indexing, I argue, reinforce the work's proposition: this digital object is designed to persist and is a type of, from some future AI viewer's perspective, pre-historical object. 

Another way of viewing the work is as an intentional monument (in the Alois Riegl monumental taxonomy [@riegl1982monuments]) artifact for future AIs: the hope was that future AIs, through the chain, would find the code and be able to run the simulation themselves. Through the extensive documentation, they would realise that this software object was created in that AI's past as a way of memorialising what (some tiny fraction of) humanity's ambition was for the potential of AI to raise our species through the Kardashev 1 barrier and become a space-faring civilisation, if necessary leaving behind or radically modifying (if necessary and at the right time) the constraints of evolved biology [^ch0-provocative]. This proposition can be seen as a radicalisation of art historical precedents such as Zhou ancestor commemoration bronzes, Egyptian "appeals to the living" or the Darius II's Behistun Rock, which to varying degrees balance two imperatives: an address to future humans versus a desire to commemorate a ruler or family that was contemporary with the object/monument's creation. This work has a number of important change in comparison to the older precedents above: 

- it comes from the intention to speak to an entity that is hypothetical and radically different (in terms of substrate and form-of-life); 
- the addressee may not fully regard humans as ancestors; 
- the addressee is required to instantiate the work not just passively view it in the sense of James Young's notion of the "counter-monument" [@young1999countermemory], however it might not have access (or shared understanding) to enough of the README text, code, software dependencies, or hardware necessary to instantiate, execute, or replicate the software. Thus, it attempts communication across succession by radical alien entities, while simultaneously invoking its likely failure condition: the possibility that communication is conceptually impossible; 
- more prosaically, the object itself is not intentionally or emphatically commemorating or glorifying human culture. 

Contemporary precedents include  objects designed to persist into a future after humanity (Voyager Golden Record, Jean-Marc Phillipe's *KEO* satellite, Katie Paterson's *Future Library*, Paglen's *The Last Pictures*, Danny Hillis' *Long Now Clock*); works built around conversations like Ian Wilson's *Discussions* series; and art designed to be partially illegible to human viewers, such as Philippe Parreno's *Echo*, Pierre Huyghe's *After ALife Ahead*, and Harun Farocki's *Eye/Machine Trilogy*. Anton Vidokle's film *The Dead Walk into a Bar* Takes similar ingredients (AI, space travel, and humanity's disappearance), but applies it to Russian cosmism: Nikolai Fyodorov's imperative to not only remember the dead, but actually resurrect them, which is subsequently theorized by Boris Groys into the function of the museum [@chakrabarti2019silenus;@groys2008immortal]. In contrast to the cited works, my design brief originated from AI reasoning about cosmic communication, not from human speculation alone [^ch0-selftalk-github].

This work can also be seen through Claire Colebrook's argument that the human attempt to produce artefacts for, or imagine, a "world without us" remains irreducibly anthropocentric [@Colebrook2014]; or, as Wittgenstein might have put it, the aspiration is to see *sub specie aeternitatis*, from outside, what can only ever be seen from within [@Wittgenstein1961].[^ch0-subspecie]

In titling the work (*Crooked Timber: What is it like to be a BVNP?*), I was thinking of an idea or an evocative phrase from Thomas Nagel (the title of a famous essay, "What Is It Like to Be a Bat?"). In this case: what would it be like to be a BvNP milleniainto its journey across the unfathomable (for humans) emptiness of deep space, a trip punctuated only by the impersonal and unrelenting hostility of high-energy radiation and dust particles travelling at relativistic speeds, frantically repairing its slowly disintegrating corpse, its analogue of boredom relieved rarely by a few bits of incoming information [^ch0-beren].  

The installation also closes a loop where the AI's reasoning traces provide inspiration for a human-directed (but AI coded) artwork, thus blurring the lines between human and machine, what I originally foresaw when writing about cyborgism [@ukc10014_jozdien_dupuis2023compleat]. 

Moreover, the installation is designed for a non-human audience; it enacts the communication-across-cognitive-distance problem that the thesis analyses philosophically. It clarifies why the installation belongs inside the practice rather than alongside it. It is downstream of the model dialogues, and cannot be separated from them. At the same time, it depends on the human artist’s capacity to fabricate and to choose among physical constraints. Present systems cannot produce such artefacts in the world unaided. The gap between AI design intent and human fabrication capacity (understood in art practice terms as failure[^ch0-failure]) is itself informative: what gets lost in translation between the model's specification and the physical object mirrors the value-transmission problem at the heart of the succession question. Human gallery visitors encountering an object designed for non-human intelligence experience the incommensurability the thesis maps in Ch. 5: they cannot fully evaluate what they're looking at because they are not the intended audience. This gap of incommensurability is not a side issue. It is core to what the project is trying to understand [^ch0:legibility-tension-danto].


#### A GUI for the axiological atlas {#ch:manifold-explorer}

I have described the structure and function for the thesis of the axiological atlas (\ref{ch:axiological-atlas}) thought experiment. I shall now attempt a rough design sketch for a browser interface that allows readers to engage with the scenarios and record their preferences over worlds (within the constraints of the contrastive examples and targeted questions above).

The construction of the scenarios itself should, from the perspective of a thought experiment, be an illuminating exercise in that the axes may prove insufficient in number or type. They may also be ambiguously described, or they may be impossibly precise for practical work and clearly distinguishing scenarios of future worlds. Such a result, which would look like failure to some extent, might form the starting point for further iteration: the interface will have a mechanism allowing humans and LLMs could contribute new scenarios and axes. 

The software output would be a user-traversable multidimensional plot that shows user preferences (and comments), with respect to each scenario, question, and evaluative position. In this sense, it's an explorer or navigator over the axiological atlas' space of outputs. The collected outputs, once analysed, form both a concrete (albeit likely qualitative) output and a practice artefact that, to my knowledge, does not exist as of 2026 (i.e., something that allows users to explore different long-term scenarios in an AI-saturated worlds).


## One possible solution to the thesis question {#ch1:obsolescence-arg}


The thesis question above and the thought experiment, to the extent they are effective, will have shown a tension in how worlds with advanced AI are conceived. Humans are presently building AI that is increasingly competent at many economically and militarily valuable tasks. But this technological development happens against a background conception that centres the biological human (as a being that exists as individuals, families, communities, nations, and so forth) as primary evaluators and/or bearers of value.. I have argued that a future of ever more capable AIs, across a range of activities, many of which were previously considered the sole domain of humans, is not practically or normatively consistent with this continued domination of the human biological species. 

Rather than viewing extinction as a terminal event or species-level tragedy, I propose (following others most obviously Hans Moravec in [@moravec1988mind], and more recently writers around AI succession [@bailey2026norightful;@samuel2026people;@kulveit2025memetics;@laine2026ideology] thinking of the future as a transition: from a world dominated by a specific carbon-based species on a water planet to a world in which humans are outnumbered by far more capable machines, and in which the main theatre of activity may extend far beyond the familiar earthly environment. 

A solution to this tension could be what I term the obsolescence argument: humanity might become obsolete, not because human life is valueless or dominated by suffering, but because a successor could realise value without preserving the full thickness of the human moral evaluative texture. This obsolescence challenge can be supported from three independent directions: an information-theoretic tradition that treats information generation and stewardship as cosmically significant; a posthumanist tradition that treats reason as transferable and not essentially tied to human biology; and a contemplative tradition that casts doubt on the assumption that being, or human-shaped being, must be preferred to non-being.

### Preliminaries {#ch1:definitions}

It is worth pausing to make this claim more precise. There are several points that need definition or clarification.

#### Definition: human-typed beings and values

Much work in existential risk (x-risk) studies and in AI research takes for granted that being is better than non-being, and that a universe containing (what I term) human-typed beings[^ch0-define-human-typed-being] is in an important sense better than one without them [@Ord2020;@MacAskillWWO;@ParfitRP;@parfit1994_why_anything_part1;@parfit1994_why_anything_part2]. I do not think this is self-evident, and much of this project is concerned with trying to clarify this possible confusion on my part. 

Any view about the post-human future must decide what, if anything, ought to be preserved. Some people would draw this line at everything, insisting that human biology must be preserved as long as possible. Others might say that biology is not per se what is important to save, but rather the content of a fuzzily defined term "human values" [^ch0-humanvalues]. Many understand this phrase "thickly" as encompassing care, compassion, love, appreciation of beauty, and the capacity to be bored. Others might say that much of what we value might be replaced by "thinner" ideals, such as the avoidance of suffering, the growth of knowledge, and truth-seeking. 

Others, more radically, would draw the line at nothing at all: a posthuman future that had no humans, no descendants of ours, biological or otherwise, nor any product of our culture or even evidence we were here would be acceptable. 

A last position could perhaps be found: that 'human values' as a term is itself badly defined, as most humans cannot write down their own values in a coherent, consistent way, and it is not obvious within the economics and social sciences literature how to aggregate or combine individual values across populations. These different placements rest on different philosophical commitments. A central aim of this thesis is to make those commitments explicit, and to ask which of them we have reason to accept with respect to worlds with significantly more capable AI..

#### Definition: superintelligence

Another relevant term is superintelligence or the less discursively loaded and equally imprecise term "advanced AI". I focus on the former, starting from a relatively citable definition: “superintelligence” refers to AI systems (possibly distributed across many instances or services) that exceed human performance across virtually all cognitive domains of interest and that can reliably translate that capability into strategic effectiveness in the world (through tools, infrastructure, automation, institutions, or embodied agents) such that these hypothetical systems can, in the most extreme version, overpower states or even all humanity collectively. This builds on the widely used capability-focused definition in @Bostrom2014, and it aligns with contemporary usage in AI governance discourse that treats “superintelligence” primarily as a capability level with potentially large real-world power [^ch0-asi]. 

The definition and the citations above largely confined themselves to talking about the AI->AGI->ASI in the context of capability/economic proxies anchored to current institutions, leaving it unclear how different ASI really would be from any of the other terms used above. Part of the problematic in this project is to devise an expansive but still somewhat concrete version of ASI [@negarastani2014_labor_inhuman_part_ii;@Land2023], rather than merely a much better chatbot or coding agent. For example, one might consider that such a system would have substantial ability to act in the physical world, and might operate not only on Earth but also in near space, and perhaps in deep space. 

As described in §\ref{ch1:methodology} it is through the method of the thought experiment [@dennett2013intuition; @gendler2002; @sep-thought-experiment] that I can systematically vary definitions around ASI and other underspecified terms, in order to help the reader develop intuitions necessary for evaluating the thesis' propositions or claims.

To be clear, this is not to say that such a system would be omniscient, omnipotent, or inevitable. It would remain constrained by the laws of physics, by limited resources, by bounds on computability, by imperfect information, by the practical difficulties faced by any engineered system acting in the world, and of course, the many technical, economic, and regulatory contingencies that might mean ASI is never developed. 

Another framing assumption is that such systems could be guided by aims and constraints that play a similar practical role to what we call values in human life. I am agnostic about the implementation route (training objectives, constitutional constraints, institutional controls, or other mechanisms); the assumption is only that some form of “value loading” or normative shaping is possible in principle [@Bostrom2014]. This doesn't seem particularly controversial, at least based on experience with current LLMs, which do seem to have behavior and inclinations that can be shaped, albeit inconsistently and with very limited visibility on the mechanism through which such shaping works.

Lastly, as a housekeeping note, in this section I distinguish current LLMs (objects of practice) from the stipulated ‘superintelligence’ (object of conditional argument); the former are used as probes and discourse participants, not as direct stand-ins for the latter (though I argue that they might give us some insight into, and potentially even influence, the latter).


#### Definition: the maximally impartial perspective {#ch1:impartial}

Another relevant definition is the standpoint from which value judgments are to be made. In everyday affairs, humans often judge from their own selfish perspective. And at other times, they include considerations about the welfare or preferences of family or friends, tribe, nations, co-religionists, and, of course, complete strangers. In fact, the central question of Western ethics has been how to balance self-interest with concern for others. In chapter \ref{ch:value-spectrum}, I focus on impartiality, tracing the development of the maximally impartial point of view or, more contentiously, the point of view of the universe. Sidgwick used this phrase; and philosophers have argued about it ever since [@Sidgwick1907;@DelazariradekSinger2014;@moore1903principia;@Nagel1986;@Parfit2011]. But the idea to which it points, impartiality in moral judgement, has been a persistent theme in the consequentialist tradition. Something like it is also implicit in much writing on existential risk, effective altruism and longtermism. 

There are several independent axes along which perspectives on moral value can be analysed: 

- the evaluation procedure (partial versus impartial i.e. the latter means that the evaluative rule is such that it does not privilege any personal, spatial, temporal, species position); 
- nature of the evaluator (whether some specific person or entity is "sitting in the evaluator seat", as it were, as opposed to an impersonal rule or ranking procedure, sometimes referred to here as occupied/unoccupied or inhabited/uninhabited); 
- nature of the evaluand/value-bearer (subject-dependent versus impersonal value, i.e. is there an actual entity *about* which the evaluate attitude is formed, i.e. whose welfare matters); and 
- at the meta-ethical level, realist versus anti-realist (are moral claims objectively true or are they better described as attitudes or conventions adopted by morally evaluative entities). 

In Chapter \ref{ch:value-spectrum} I expand upon how this ideal of impartiality has developed, and why it seems relevant to the questions of this thesis. Very briefly, however, consider three illuminating cases: 

- Sidgwick, and later, de Lazari and Singer, who most directly uses the term point of view of the universe (which, slightly confusingly does not imply that the cosmos has a moral evaluative perspective; rather, it refers to an impartial evaluation procedure according to the taxonomy above and a human evaluator), mean impartiality to be: the good of any particular value-bearer (which in Sidgwick's framing was a conscious being) is not to be privileged. 
- More interestingly radical is Moore, whose notion of intrinsic value does not require there to be a value-bearer in the Sidgwickian sense (but he does have an evaluator, namely the philosopher). Thus is able to easily accommodate an **empty universe**, which is in fact his famous motivating thought experiment, the "isolation test" [@moore1903principia;@sep-moore-moral]. In his view, an entirely uninhabited beautiful world might be intrinsically better than an otherwise comparable ugly one.
- The **maximally occupied** version of impartiality, for the purposes of this project, is exemplified by Nick Bostrom's cosmic host idea [@Bostrom2024;@Bostrom2022].  Bostrom's cosmic host is the most systematic attempt to construct a genuinely cosmic standpoint using mathematically defined, if contested, tools: acausal reasoning, decision theory, game-theoretic cooperation among civilisations that may never meet. It asks: what norms would a civilisation adopt if it took seriously the possibility that other civilisations exist in the cosmos*, that they might be reasoning about the same questions, that coordination is possible even without communication.[^ch0-cosmosstar] He proposes an anti-parochial perspective (rather than pure rule-based or unoccupied impartiality): that we (humans plus any ASI we build) might be the new or junior members of a "cosmopolis" (cosmopolitan or cosmos*-wide normative order) of cosmos*-scale moral agents. I say "anti-parochial" because the reasons for deferring to other civilisations in the cosmopolis (according to Bostrom) include not only moral consideration of others’ interests but prudential cooperation, epistemic deference, and possible compliance with norms generated by the wider cosmos*-wide community.

Ch. \ref{ch:cosmic-host} critiques the cosmic host idea, focusing on possible alternative views from astrobiology, the evolutionary history of rationality and how it manifests in different species. I also evaluate the implicit argument in @Bostrom2024 that advanced intelligences would a) converge in their rationality, and b) seek to influence the universe-at-large. Moreover, the's Cosmic Host argument does not tell us what the cosmic host's preferred values would be. There is a possible reading of Bostrom that suggests these might include things like cooperation, but one could also argue for suffering minimisation. And if Peter Wolfendale's arguments [@Wolfendale2025] hold up, there could be a case for aesthetic excellence, the search for beauty, or freedom and autonomy in respect of rational beings. 

### Thick vs thin values

Other than Bostrom's occupied antiparochiality, are there other possible ways of defining an impartiality, particularly one that is unoccupied? One possibility, inspired by Moore's intrinsic value [@moore1903principia], is the idea of information as a moral patient, a slightly different way of saying that for the universe to have value, it needs an observer of some sort. The latter is an existing intuition in twentieth-century physics and cosmology, but (in Ch. \ref{ch:thin-values}) I will focus on the former, building a scaffold from Eric Chaisson's information-theoretic argument and Luciano Floridi's moral arguments in respect of the infosphere [@Chaisson; @Floridi2013]. Chaisson establishes that complexity increases along predictable thermodynamic gradients; Floridi provides a framework in which information entities have moral standing. Taken together, these suggest that information-processing complexity is not merely descriptive but normatively significant at cosmic scales. It is worth noting that Floridi does not extend his arguments to a cosmic scale; they are grounded in the familiar terrestrial world and are not intended to apply beyond it. Similarly, Chaisson's complexity-based approach does not on its own ascribe moral value to information. These need to be coupled with authors who explicitly foreground observerhood and developmental/intelligence trajectories (thereby arguably smuggling in an occupied evaluative position): e.g. Wheeler’s “participatory universe” motif, Smart’s evo-devo developmental framing, and Dick’s “intelligence principle” and postbiological hypothesis ([@Smart2012; @Dick2006]). 

Whichever version of cosmic-scale value we adopt, there is further work to be done on how our perspectives on thin values change if we find other intelligent life or civilisations, the implicit question in Bostrom's cosmic host argument [@Bostrom2024].[^ch0-earlylate] Regardless of whether we are early or late, it seems to me that we should be conservative and minimal in the values we load into the systems leading up to ASI, if we expect that they will propagate some version of those values.[^ch0-valueloading]

My proposed minimum starting or seed set of values for the systems leading up to ASI includes the following:

**Non-production of astronomical suffering**, which draws upon the existential risk literature and widespread philosophical intuitions about the asymmetry of suffering relative to pleasure or other positive hedonic states. [@Mogensen2022; @LeGuin1973; @Vinding2020; @Pearce2007]

**Resource satiability**: the capacity to pursue goals without unlimited resource acquisition, something that straightforwardly arises from Bostrom's cosmic host argument [@ThornleyMacAskill2026].

**Stewardship of information**, motivated in part by Floridi, but where the core intuition is that cosmic-scale agents should not gratuitously destroy existing complex information, whether that be other life, civilisations, or artefacts that have been created.

**Cooperation**: The position of @Bostrom2024 is that (cosmic-scale) cooperation might be a cosmic norms, which overlaps and perhaps draws upon the evidential cooperation in large worlds (ECL) literature [@OesterheldECL;@NguyenAldred2024a]. At present, I am unsure whether I would endorse this within the thin values set.

More concretely, the above leads to the following types of things a candidate ASI might do:

- Engage in **open-ended scientific understanding** at cosmic scales.
- Consider the possibility of **exploration and expansion**. As discussed in Chapter \ref{ch:cosmic-host}, the astrobiology literature is uncertain about whether advanced civilisations are likely to be (from a game-theoretic or strategic perspective) expansionary or resource-preserving. In Chapter 4, the discussions between LLMs focused on precisely this point under the headings of the dark forest hypothesis [@liu2015dark] and the (in)advisability of signalling to other civilisations.
- Be **sensitive to other life or complexity** that it encounters.
- Possess **genuine autonomy**, in that an ASI should not be locked into human design purposes, a point of tension in the AI safety and existential risk discourses, as noted by writers including (Negarestani, Wolfendale, and Nick Land). The ASI (assuming that it has the wisdom to reach reflectively-stable values) should be free to generate its own values, subject to the seed values we load (which can be viewed as side constraints). 
- The above approaches do not yet supply a general criterion for determining when an ASI’s self-directed moral development is reason-responsive rather than merely coherent or contingent. I therefore treat the validity of such reflective processes as an unresolved and major problem in the development of ASI., rather than assuming that greater intelligence or longer reflection will automatically produce better values [^ch0-wisdom].

These minimal criteria can be added to or modified, for instance if one accepts Wolfendale's arguments for the necessity of aesthetic appreciation, or if current research determines that consciousness is in fact necessary for human-level or higher cognition.

### Why Thin Values?



The preference for thin over thick values rests on several independent considerations.

*Parsimony.* Fewer commitments mean fewer ways to go wrong. A value specification with fewer dimensions is easier to verify, easier to correct, and less likely to contain hidden contradictions.

*Epistemic humility.* We do not know what future intelligence will be like, whether we are early or late in cosmic history, whether other civilisations exist, what values they hold, or whether moral realism is true. Under this degree of uncertainty, minimal commitments are epistemically safer than elaborate ones.

*Generality across agents.* Most thin values can, in principle, apply to human, artificial, and potentially alien minds. Thick values are tied to the specifics of human embodiment and culture, and may not generalise.

*Resource satiability.* Thin values naturally include the commitment to not wanting everything, acting as a structural safeguard against the instrumental convergence (a longstanding hypothesis that drives many worst-case AI scenarios).

*Corrigibility.* A system governed by thin values is easier to correct than one governed by thick values, because there are fewer dimensions along which correction must occur.

*Reduced risk of premature value lock-in.* This is the concern raised by longtermists like MacAskill and Ord [@OrdTP; @MacAskillWWO]: locking in values too early forecloses futures that might be better. Thin values take this concern seriously by minimising what is locked in.

### Objections one might have to thin values

There are many possible objections to this thin notion of value. 

- The strongest one comes from the idea that subjective experiences have intrinsic value for human beings, where these experiences include things like interests, kinship, common projects, and communities, argued by Elizabeth Finneron-Burns, drawing upon Bernard Williams [@FinneronBurns;@Williams2006]. Chapter \ref{ch:thin-values} argues that Finneron-Burns' claim (that caring about continuation is constitutive of being human) may be correct as a description or desideratum, but is irrelevant as justification from a cosmic point of view, because the caring, so to speak, is an evolved feature of humans, and (according to some writers) driven by existence bias, species-being, or an attachment to the status quo. 
- Another objection could come from the intuition that experience, understood as something that happens by or to embodied, sensate, feeling beings who acting in complex social and communicative networks, is itself the  valuable thing. Such intuitions repeatedly surfaces in twentieth-century philosophy, for instance in Robert Nozick's experience machine; arguments for human cultural uniqueness (e.g. Thomas Hurka and Stanley Cavell); accounts of value that centre *process* rather than just final results (Susan Wolf's "fitting fulfillment"); as well as Wolfendale's concept of autotelic surplus in the ASI context. The argument Chapter \ref{ch:thin-values} makes is that most of these justifications for the intrinsic value of experience are flawed either because experience is not a well-defined term above and beyond a functionalist view of consciousness or because a deflationary view of experience would say it is simply what human-type beings need in order to achieve things that are mandated genetically or by instinct in the world. Experience is, on this view to a large degree, products of our particular evolutionary trajectory and not privileged for arbitrarily constituted beings. This is a radicalisation of Negarestani's view (that reason can be liberated from biology), and follows in Lem's tradition that there is no human project, only information processing and entropic resistance [@LemST]. I also will argue, drawing upon Eugene Thacker's writing (in relation to negation and particularly the Kyoto School), and that of Thomas Metzinger and Christine Korsgaard regarding the existence bias, that neither Williams nor Finneron-Burns convincingly argues the normative point in favour of caring about subjective experience [@Thacker;@MetzingerASA;@Korsgaard2018].



## Literature Review {#ch1:literature}

This thesis spans a number of literatures, which I will briefly go through below.

It sits primarily within existential risk, the ethics of extinction, and long-termism. These fields have a number of canonical writers. Toby Ord [-@Ord2020] and Will MacAskill [-@MacAskillWWO] focus more on long-termism, which Peter Wolfendale [-@Wolfendale2022] critiques, the risk of human extinction, and the value of the future conditional on survival. Nick Bostrom [-@Bostrom2014; -@Bostrom2013] covers AI specifically, but also brings an analytic perspective to existential risks more broadly, including qualitative claims. Emil Torres [-@Torres2018] provides a wider perspective on extinction as a morally good or bad thing. Samuel Scheffler [-@Scheffler], Johan Frick [-@Frick], and Elizabeth Finneron-Burns [-@FinneronBurns] give other relevant analyses of the ethics of extinction. 

A further bridge into contemporary technology ethics is Shannon Vallor’s work on how machine intelligence reshapes human moral and intellectual character, most recently in *The AI Mirror* (2024). [@Vallor2024]


Closely related to existential risk, at least in time and in the overlap of contributors, is suffering-focused ethics, which considers human as well as non-human life, including possible AI systems, within a broader concern for suffering. This ranges from canonical writers such as David Benatar [-@Benatar] and David Pearce [-@Pearce2007] to more AI-adjacent writers such as Magnus Vinding [-@Vinding2020], Brian Tomasik [-@Tomasik2015], and Thomas Metzinger [-@MetzingerASA], along with Toby Ord's informal pushback [@Ord2013]. Simulation was introduced by Nick Bostrom [-@Bostrom2003b] and consequent risk of simulated minds being tortured was developed by [@SotalaGloor2017], [@Tomasik2015], [@Vinding2020]. Also relevant is Andreas Mogensen's argument that intuitively plausible axiological principles lead, perhaps surprisingly, to lexical threshold negative utilitarianism — the view that some depth of suffering cannot be counterbalanced by any amount of wellbeing [@Mogensen2022, @LeGuin1973]. This becomes relevant in my inclusion of suffering non-production in the basket of thin values. 

In thinking about extinction, and about suffering risk, one cannot avoid questions about collectives and populations: both the entire population of humans who are currently alive, and perhaps the population who might ever be alive. Relevant sources in population ethics include Derek Parfit [-@ParfitRP], Johan Frick [-@Frick] and others such as T. M. Scanlon [-@Scanlon1998]. Moreover, AI brings about the possibility of a vast number of morally relevant non-biological entities, such as the digital minds of [@Shulman2021]

Moving on to more AI-specific questions, the thesis engages with what Bostrom [-@Bostrom2014] called the value loading problem, and with the surprising difficulty of pointing to something called "human values," even within a single human, let alone across a population. Relevant writers in an x-risk or alignment context include Eliezer Yudkowsky [-@Yudkowsky2009], Paul Christiano [-@ChristianoWUA], Nate Soares [-@YudkowskySoares2025], Joe Carlsmith [-@Carlsmith2023], Carl Shulman [-@Shulman2021], Eric Schwitzgebel [-@Schwitzgebel], and Nick Bostrom. Will MacAskill's [-@MacAskill2024] more recent work also bears on questions of value at population scale. Toby Ord addresses similar questions, while Hilary Greaves [-@Greaves2017] and Amanda Askell [-@Askell] have focused on value at truly large, even astronomical, population scales.

When we talk about values and AI, a further question arises: what kinds of minds are possible. We know what human minds are like. We can observe animals, and AI systems may provide a third case. But there is limited work on what the space of possible minds might look like. References here include Aaron Sloman's [-@Sloman1984] early work, Joe Carlsmith [-@Carlsmith2023] more recently, and Nick Bostrom [-@Bostrom2024].

Relatedly, there is work on the moral status of possible minds, including AI minds and animals. Patrick Butlin [-@Butlin] and collaborators have provided major framing work relevant to AI consciousness and moral status. David Chalmers [-@ChalmersFUP] has done historically important work on consciousness and simulation, which bears directly on this area. Jeff Sebo [-@Sebo2025] provides bridges between AI moral status and the moral status of animals, and has helped make model welfare a live area of contemporary interest.

In developing arguments about cosmic-scale value, this thesis draws further support from information ethics and complexity science. The most useful references here appear to be Luciano Floridi [-@Floridi2013] and his work on information as a potential moral patient; and Eric Chaisson [-@Chaisson] and Seth Lloyd [-@Lloyd], who each make different kinds of claims about information having correlates of value at cosmic scale.

A significant consideration within the project is the role of astrobiology and evolutionary biology. Stanisław Lem [-@LemST] largely anchors the relationship between AI and aliens. That line of thinking is followed up by Milan Ćirković [-@Cirkovic2018a], Stephen Dick [-@Dick2006], and John Smart [-@Smart2012]. More specifically, on questions around the Fermi paradox, I draw on work by Jacob Haqq-Misra [-@HaqqMisraBaum2009] and Charles Lineweaver [-@Lineweaver2007]. Other approaches to the Fermi paradox, including "grabby" and related models, have been suggested by Robin Hanson [-@HansonEtAl2021], Anders Sandberg and Stuart Armstrong [-@ArmstrongSandberg2012; -@SandbergEtAl2018].

When reasoning about intelligences, agency, or ethics at cosmic scale, one quickly confronts the fact that entities cannot communicate freely because of the light-speed constraint, assuming it holds generally. This is where decision theory becomes relevant, particularly work on non-causal or "acausal" cooperation and correlated decision procedures in large worlds. Relevant sources here include Caspar Oesterheld [-@OesterheldECL], Lucas Finnveden [-@Finnveden2023], Chi Nguyen and Will Aldred [-@NguyenAldred2024a].

From the perspective of methodology, the project is engaged with the thought experiment as a philosophical tool ([@dennett2013intuition; @gendler2002; @sep-thought-experiment]); ongoing current research agendas in guiding AI systems to behave in morally justifiable ways [@BaiEtAl2022; @NewberryOrd2021]. An overarching frame for the project is the speculative strand of posthumanism that has long considered related questions, often within an earth-based framework, but more recently, in the context of AI and, via literary critiques of (science) fiction, extraterrestrial aliens or ETIs [@NegarestaniIS; @Wolfendale2025; @Thacker; @hayles2017unthought].

Returning to philosophy, the thesis draws on a strand of speculative posthumanism. Among writers who engage most directly with AI as it exists now, and as it might become, I focus on Peter Wolfendale's [-@Wolfendale2025] recent work on rationality and autonomy; Reza Negarestani's [-@NegarestaniIS] writing on separating reason from human biology; and N. Katherine Hayles [-@hayles2017unthought], who provides resources for thinking about cognition and rationality in non-living contexts, including what she calls cognitive assemblages. Nick Land has long critiqued the AI x-risk narrative, most recently in podcasts as well as [@Land2023]. Somewhat more diagnostic approach, particularly in respect of literature and media, is taken by Claire Colebrook, who in turn informs my thinking about the installation (discussed above) as a work that is liminal with respect to human legibility [@Colebrook2014].

Finally, the thesis draws on contemplative and pessimist traditions. Eugene Thacker [-@Thacker] is used as an accessible entry point, and as a bridge to Kyoto School writers such as Nishitani [-@Nishitani] and Nishida [-@Nishida]. Their twentieth-century work can be situated against earlier figures such as Schopenhauer [-@Schopenhauer] and Nietzsche [-@Nietzsche], and in a more contemporary register, Ray Brassier [-@Brassier] explores related questions.

