[^ch0-wisdom]: The term "wisdom" is often used in longtermist and AI-related writing [johnson2026wise;carlsmith2025alignment;carlsmith2026humanlikephilosophy;@benthamsbulldog2026ai;@benthamsbulldog2026b], but what does this actually mean in an ASI context? That is, what conditions need obtain before which we might regard a successor’s moral reflection as trustworthy? Revisability (of values) and reflective stability (a narrower or more technical usage within early AI alignment, which can be summarised as "would AI would modify its own values once it was more capable and if it had the option") seem inadequate since arbitrary or bad values might also be stable under reflection or self-modification. Relevant literatures approach parts of this problem from different directions (some of which are longstanding foreseen issues in AI alignment, while others are emerging as LLMs approach what some regard as AGI-level). Iason Gabriel distinguishes alignment with instructions, preferences, ideal preferences, interests, and values [@gabriel2020values], while recent critiques of preference-based alignment question whether coherent preference satisfaction is an adequate model of normative agency at all [@tan2024beyond]. Coherent Extrapolated Volition (CEV) attempts to specify an idealising process through which values might be revised under greater information, cognitive capacity, self-understanding, and deliberation [@yudkowsky2004cev;@Bostrom2014;@Moret2023]. Wide reflective equilibrium extends the philosophical notion of Rawlsian reflective equilibrium to the LLM context [@brophy2026wre;@sep-reflective-equilibrium]. The moral-uncertainty literature cautions against premature convergence where normative uncertainty remains unresolved [@macaskill2020moral]; and writing on corrigibility asks how increasingly capable agents might remain open to legitimate correction rather than locking in their current objectives [@holtman2019corrigibility].



[^ch0-digitalminds]: It is sometimes objected that current AI systems, being disembodied, cannot suffer and therefore lack moral status. This conflates embodiment with sentience. The question of whether AI systems are or could become moral patients turns on whether they have morally relevant experiences (particularly sentience or phenomenal consciousness), not on whether they have bodies. The most systematic treatment to date is Butlin et al.'s report on consciousness in AI, which surveys leading theories of consciousness and finds that no current system is a strong candidate for consciousness but that none can be categorically ruled out [@Butlin;Butlin2026488;Ladak2024;Seth2024]. Along similar lines, Sebo [-@Sebo2025] and Sebo and Long [-@SeboLong2023] argue that moral consideration for AI systems is a live and urgent question, and that the probability of moral patiency, even if currently low for large language models, is non-negligible and rising as architectures grow more complex. The prudent position is that AI moral patiency cannot be dismissed on the basis of substrate alone. Thus, the moral justifications are uncertain rather than absent: we do not yet have the conceptual toolkit for determining whether current LLMs or future AIs are moral patients to whom we have actual ethical obligations. But uncertainty about patienthood does not entail the absence of obligation; under moral uncertainty, a non-trivial probability of patienthood can ground duties of care in expectation. I therefore term this a *quasi-obligation*: not because we cannot owe anything, but because we do not yet know whether, or how much, we do. There might also be prudential reasons that sit alongside moral ones, with the approximate flavour of "treat AIs well so they treat us well in the future", or of setting good precedents (Bostrom & Shulman 2022). Also relevant: [@Goodin2007] is a canonical source on consulting affected parties, in theory of democracy; [@SalibGoldstein2024] gives a more prudential gloss, discussing limited economic and other rights for AIs; [@FinnvedenTaylorDalton2026] discuss how we can be honest with AIs; [@ThornleyMacAskill2026] as well as [@StastnyJarviniemiShlegeris2025] discuss how we might bargain with AIs.)

[^ch0-earlylate]: The cosmic host framework raises a crucial uncertainty that is central to Bostrom's argument and this project: are we early or late in cosmic history? Bostrom implies we are probably late, that an existing community of advanced civilisations has established norms. This is a position somewhat supported by [@HansonEtAl2021; @Sandberg2021; @ArmstrongSandberg2012] and to some extent [@SnyderBeattieEtAl2021]. The astrobiology literature [@CirkovicTGS; @Smart2012] suggests we might be relatively early. This uncertainty has direct implications for what sort of values that humanity, which (by stipulation according to Bostrom) a civilisation on the cusp of technological maturity, bequeath to peer or less-mature civilsations. If humanity is late then thick human values risk being parochial relative to established cosmic norms. Thin values are more likely to be compatible with whatever norms already exist. If we are early, then thick human values risk locking in one evolutionary accident as cosmic precedent. A thin set of values can also perhaps be augmented as epistemic uncertainty reduces. In either case, the prudent move is to mature and seek understanding before propagating value-content. 

[^ch0-beren]: These speculations, I should note, are not entirely fanciful: the challenges of AI-piloted space travel were discussed in a somewhat quantitatively concrete post by @beren2026colonizing.

[^ch0-cosmosstar]: Recall that "cosmos*" denotes the causally reachable universe, the multiverse (if any), as well as simulations we may be embedded in, which is the wide, ontologically-uncertain scope that Bostrom uses in @Bostrom2024.

[^ch0-selftalk-github]: Model self-talk logs available here, with an HTML viewer/Jupyter notebook in the enclosing repository: https://github.com/ukc10014/cosmichost_mp/tree/main/logs/logs_selftalk

[^ch0-valueloading]: There are other complexities: if we believe we are building ASI that will propagate its values, is it prudentially or morally advisable to build ASI at all (to the extent that it influences other civilisations inappropriately or communicates our presence to hostile ETIs); what confidence do we have that whatever values ASI ultimately generate for itself/themselves are, in a relevant moral or prudential sense, better than the values that humans have come up with.

[^ch0-provocative]: This framing is deliberately provocative: it invokes the critical perspectives such as from Torres and Gebru [@GebruTorres2024] discussed above in §\ref{ch1:critical-ai}. It is also precisely the negative terminal scenario portrayed in an influential and detailed report circa-2025 on how current LLM-based systems might develop to AGI and beyond [@KokotajloEtAl2025].

[^ch0:safety-ethics-alignment]: AI safety, ethics, and alignment are substantially different subsets of the broader AI discourse, although that ongoing historical procession is not this project's topic.

[^ch0-glissant]: The interaction has a Glissantian structure: the model is opaque in Glissant's sense (albeit not intentionally; rather, we simply don't understand the cognition of LLMs very well), while the training corpus constitutes a kind of Tout-Monde, a totality we engage but cannot claim to possess or sum up [@glissant1997poetics].

[^ch0-anthropo]: It should be noted that a significant amount of the relevant research in this field, in decision theory, anthropics, and empirical AI, attempts to find mathematical or falsifiable approaches to research questions. In fact, this operates as a selection filter for the problems researchers choose to work on.

[^ch0-analytic]: To qualify a seemingly sweeping statement, in my review of impartiality (Chapter 2), it will become clearer that longtermism, existential risk, and suffering risk studies, the primary fields with which this project engages, need some way of ascribing value that is mathematically tractable. To fill this need, they use a consequentialist or utilitarian framework. Typically, they come to some notion of welfare (historically for humans but more recently for nonhuman animals as well as potentially AI systems as [@Sebo2025] discusses). This idea of welfare needs to be operationalised; examples include quality-adjusted life years in the policy realm, utility functions in a more abstract sense, as well as the moral parliament approach discussed in Ch. \ref{ch:empirical-host}. However, this quantitative approach struggles with precisely the worlds of this project: ones where there are no entities, or an infinite number of entities, to which utility can be assigned. Parfit's work on personal identity and population ethics, particularly the non-identity problem and the repugnant conclusion, mapped these failures. The point is not merely technical. These breakdowns reveal an embedded anthropocentric assumption: that for a world to have moral weight, it must contain welfare-bearing entities legible to human evaluative frameworks. The Kyoto School's treatment of śūnyatā (emptiness) as a philosophical starting-point rather than a deficit offers an alternative.

[^ch0-parfitlrb]: Parfit treated the topic of why anything exists in a two-part article in the London Review of Books. His articles were a return to the classical philosophical question of causation and explanations for the existence or otherwise of God. Rather than asking whether God caused reality, Parfit asks whether any feature of reality (specifically, simplicity, fullness, mathematical beauty) might explain why this particular cosmic possibility obtained rather than another. His conclusion is that the simplest meta-level explanation is that there is no selector at all: reality simply exists as a brute fact. This analytic treatment of the reason for existence is a counterpart to Thacker's attempt to reconcile or "dwell in" the idea of nothingness.

[^ch0-humanvalues]: Following well-cited sources in AI research and safety [@gabriel_artificial_2020;@RussellHC], the term "human values" is used throughout this thesis as shorthand for the cluster of normative commitments, preferences, and evaluative dispositions characteristic of human beings as a species. I am aware that this is contested, particularly within AI ethics, where the immediate question is: whose values? The term is underspecified in the sense that values are bound to individuals, cultures, and historical moments, and no single formulation could represent eight billion people. Nevertheless, the term remains useful at the level of abstraction at which this thesis operates. The relevant contrast is not between one human culture's values and another's, but between human-type values taken as a class and the values that a radically different mind (an ASI or an extraterrestrial intelligence) might hold. My background assumption (which I will not defend) is that at my chosen scale of comparison, the differences among human value systems are small relative to the distance between any human value system and a truly alien one. Where precision matters, the thesis distinguishes between thick values (culturally specific, richly textured) and thin values (abstract principles that might generalise across mind types), a distinction developed in Chapter 5, and provisionally presented here. For a specific approach to the related question of how to elicit and reconcile human values in ways that do not run into well-known choices in preference-based or quantitative aggregation frameworks see Oliver Klingefjord et al's moral graph elicitation[@klingefjord2024humanvalues]. See also the footnote on human-typed beings [^ch0-define-human-typed-being].

[^ch0-value]: <!--TODO: get cites -->"Value" is a multivalent term used differently across disciplines. In analytic philosophy, axiology concerns what types of things have value or what constitutes the good (see Schroeder, "Value Theory," Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy). In sociology and social psychology, values (plural) refers to enduring beliefs held by individuals or populations about what is desirable or preferable (Rokeach, 1973; Schwartz, 1992). Values are sometimes conflated with preferences, a term from economics and decision theory denoting ordinal rankings over outcomes (von Neumann & Morgenstern, 1944), a conflation particularly prevalent in AI alignment literature, where "loading human values" may implicitly reduce axiological questions to preference orderings. Finally, desires and wants are psychological terms for motivational states directed at objects or outcomes, less commonly used in the alignment literature. It is worth noting that when alignment researchers invoke "human values," they typically operate closer to the sociological sense (the aggregated norms and preferences of existing human populations) rather than the axiological sense, though this is rarely made explicit. This is a substantive commitment: it presupposes that the values worth preserving are those humans currently hold, rather than those that would survive rational scrutiny or prove genuinely good across wider contexts. For the purposes of this project, "values" typically refers to the axiological sense: what is genuinely good or worth pursuing across long time frames and across cognitive distances.

[^ch0-subspecie]: From the cited, Wittgenstein writes: "The work of art is the object seen sub specie aeternitatis; and the good life is the world seen sub specie aeternitatis. This is the connexion between art and ethics." and "The usual way of looking at things sees objects as it were from the midst of them, the view sub specie aeternitatis from outside."

[^ch0-define-human-typed-being]: "Human-typed" beings is (not surprisingly) closer to a family resemblence [@WittgensteinPI] rather than a biological or essentialist classification. Under this framing, a being is human-typed insofar as its psychology, affective life, agency, sociality, or cultural inheritance remains substantially continuous with characteristic human forms, notwithstanding technological augmentation or non-biological embodiment, as well as the great diversity of current/future human beings/cultures. The term is intended to include some uploads, cyborgs, and posthuman descendants, while excluding human-derived systems that have diverged sufficiently far from human forms of cognition and evaluation. The boundary is purpose-relative and admits borderline cases; it is not intended to settle questions of numerical personal identity, species membership, consciousness, or moral status. Different writers have handled the difficulties of specifying this family resemblance. For instance, David Roden, drawing on Derridean notions of the human lack-of-essence, is open to the possibility that radically different (in functional terms as well as in  their autonomy) posthumans might nevertheless be treated as continuous with or descending from current human cultures, as our concepts and practices (art, literature, technology) acclimatise to their presence.[@roden2010deconstruction;@roden2014posthuman]. Katherine Hayles, who, in context of AIs/uploads (but pre-LLM) suggests that human  continuity should not be treated as a purely informational, dematerialised, or substrate-independent feature. She sees the human cognition and identity as something that is intimately bound up with our embodiment and our sociotechnical environments. This implies that it is entirely possible that AI's typology would be similarly bound up with their sensorium, locomotive apparatus, substrate and relational environment [@hayles1999posthuman]. How could this be operationalised or falsified? One might test AI systems trained or adapted within systematically different sensorimotor environments (as a further post-training stage) to see if they developed correspondingly different internal structures for representing agency, objects, space, causation, value, and selfhood, even where their linguistic behavior is held approximately constant. Such a test would obviously depart from how humans are actually trained: a mix of linguistic, sensorimotor and relational data (but I am trying to stay in the LLM paradigm for the example). Also useful is David Chalmers work distinguishing several forms of uploading (e.g. likely-destructive cryonic brain-slicing vs invasive or non-invasive brain-scanning) and separates questions of consciousness, psychological continuity, and personal survival [@chalmers2014uploading].


[^ch0-asitimeline]: Public forecasting tools and journalism give a rough sense of how compressed "superintelligence timelines" have become in contemporary discourse. For example, a Polymarket question on whether OpenAI will announce it has achieved AGI before 2027 was trading around the low-teens percentage range (i.e., a minority probability as of late 2025). On Manifold, a market on whether "unambiguous artificial superintelligence" will exist by 1 Jan 2030 was priced at roughly ~30% (with other ASI-by-2030 markets lower), reflecting both nontrivial expectation and substantial uncertainty. The forecasting site Metaculus, meanwhile, showed an approximately 2-year time gap between a weak AGI and superintelligence as defined in the relevant entry (see link for definitions). Meanwhile, mainstream coverage often frames timelines through named forecasters: e.g., The Guardian reported an updated forecast that set 2034 as a revised horizon for "superintelligence" (without committing to a "human destruction" date), illustrating both the salience of nearish timelines and their volatility. [@down_2026_guardian_ai_timeline_delays; @manifold_yudkowsky_asi_by_2030_2027; @polymarket_agi_before_2027_2025; @metaculus2020_weakagi_to_superintelligent_oracle]

[^ch0-asi]: It's debated whether even the lower level of capability than superintelligence (ASI), known as artificial general intelligence (AGI), has a settled, coherent definition. For instance, Melanie Mitchell explicitly frames AGI and "superintelligence" as speculative and notes that these views differ from those studying biological intelligence, i.e., the concept is contested and not settled. DeepMind’s “Levels of AGI” paper is basically motivated by definitional ambiguity: it surveys existing AGI definitions and proposes an ontology/taxonomy precisely because “AGI” is used inconsistently and is hard to benchmark. Helen Toner frames AGI as a term that is less useful now when capability levels are arguably within the range of what the term AGI is sometimes intended to convey, relative to a decade ago when it was very clearly beyond the capability of then-current models [@morris2023_levels_of_agi; @mitchell2024_debates_agi; @brennan_kak_west2025_artificial_power;toner2026agi]. Related operationalisations include: forecasters often use HLMI (Human level machine intelligence i.e. “unaided machines can accomplish every task better and more cheaply than human workers”) as a measurable proxy, and AI policy discourse often uses Transformative AI to denote systems whose effects are comparable to an industrial or agricultural revolution. Here “AGI” is used in the common sense of general-purpose AI that matches or exceeds human capability across a broad range of tasks (often proxied as “most economically valuable work”); it is typically treated as a nearer (and somewhat more operationalisable) target than “superintelligence,” which denotes a further capability regime beyond human level. [@openai_charter; @openai_2023_introducing_superalignment; @steinperlman2022_espai]

[^ch0-bostrom]: Bostrom is a central interlocutor here, but his role is contested. A racist email he wrote in the 1990s resurfaced publicly in 2023; he issued an apology and repudiation. The episode, and wider controversies around longtermism and the institutional legacy of the Future of Humanity Institute, have shaped how this body of work is received. In engaging Bostrom’s ideas, I neither ignore this context nor treat it as decisive refutation: the thesis evaluates arguments while remaining attentive to the intellectual and institutional conditions under which they became influential. [@bostrom2023_apology_old_email; @guardian2024_fhi_closure]

[^ch0-creator]: "Creator" in this context isn't deistic. It could be the architect of a software simulation, or it could be a misaligned AI in the canonical version of the paperclip maximiser: the AI's utility function happened to assign more weight to variation in paperclips, even though the dominant term in that utility function was to fill its reality with paperclips. 

[^ch0-intergalactic-spreading]: One of the more concrete specifications for how human-originated missions could rapidly, at least in cosmological terms, spread beyond the Milky Way is developed by Stuart Armstrong and Andrew Sandberg in [@ArmstrongSandberg2012]. Their exploratory-engineering study argues that a mature, highly automated civilisation could initiate settlement not merely of the Local Group but of much of the causally reachable universe. Their design is different from other von Neumann probe implementations because it does not require the starships to stop at intermediate points to replicate. Instead, their propose an enormous initial fan-out: probes are launched directly toward each reachable galaxy and, after arrival, construct local replicators and dispatch a second wave throughout that galaxy, allowing them to theoretically reach all stars that humanity can causally reach (owing to the expanding universe, most of the universe cannot be reached by ordinary, physically-plausible sub-light methods). In this document, the authors were trying to show that rapid colonisation of nearby galaxies is theoretically possible within billions of years, using technologies that humanity could foreseeably and plausibly develop over thousands or millions of years, and that the total mass and energy and mass required (to construct and launch the probes respectively) is available in the Solar System. It does not, however, discuss in detail what the implications for the AI systems running the probe might be; a subsequent, more informal treatment by Beren Milledge take more conservative assumptions (higher weight as a result of shielding against relativistic dust particles and therefore higher energy requirements, if speed is held constant, and longer launch period) and briefly discusses the high level of intelligence needed to autonomously guide and complete the mission. However, the referenced post was more concerned with how a civilisation might move from Kardashev 2 (K2) to K3, which is largely beyond the frame of interest in this project; I ask what happens as we go from our current state (approximately Kardashev 0.75) to K2.[@beren2026colonizing] 

[^ch0-moore]: For instance, though this isn't Moore's example, if the Mona Lisa were somehow transplanted or teleported to a corner of our universe that was not causally reachable by any humans or any other entity that could perceive it or that have the capacity to experience it, what would it mean for it to have value? One might hold that value require subjects who can form evaluative judgments; and so the question as stipulated is meaningless. A different view could be that its rare and intricate and intentional configuration of atoms confers a value by virtue of its structured information content. Yet another could be that the fact that it has been created through a process that is conventionally regarded as separate distinct from the natural physical evolution of the universe, and this is what gives it value. These examples, as well as Moore's, seem to presuppose an evaluative standpoint that is outside of experiencing subjects. However, it should be noted that Moore, who raises the question of intrinsic value, in fact, does not provide a detailed defense of his claim that the beautiful world would have such intrinsic value (in absence of a human viewer) other than to appeal to the reader's intuition.

[^ch0-parfit-bio]: For a vivid example of subjectivity or the situated perspective within philosophy, one might read the reviews of David Edmonds's biography of Derek Parfit, which argued that his peculiar temperament must be taken into account when assessing the *On What Matters* series that were his late works, as well as his overriding philosophical commitment to a unified moral theory [@kodsi2024imperfect]. 

[^ch0:legibility-tension-danto]: The installation confronts an inescapable tension. Art that aspires to address a nonhuman or future audience cannot simply discard the human viewer without consequences. To do so would be to abandon the materials, formalism, and discursive context that constitute it as art; that is, to fall out of Danto's "artworld", the enveloping cloud of theory and history without which something cannot be seen as art at all [@Danto1964]. The work would collapse into (or at least be hard to productively distinguish from) a datafile, a machine learning research log. Yet the opposite move is equally unavailable. The tradition of radically open works (Rauschenberg's *White Paintings*, Cage's *4'33"*) shows that when artistic intention is emptied out, the vacuum is immediately filled by the human viewer's own presence: ambient light, bodily noise, the audience hearing itself. These works are not steps toward nonhuman address but its negation, *tabulae rasae* that make the work maximally dependent on human environment and attention. The installation is thus caught between two collapses: toward pure data (where removing the human viewer removes what many might understand as the art) and toward pure receptivity (where removing the content returns everything to the human viewer). There is no stable position between these poles. We are trapped, yet again, in an anthropocentric cage, the thesis' central problematic.

[^ch0-failure]: Failure need not have the same methodological meaning across experimental practices. For instance, in science, an experiment can fail technically while succeeding epistemically, as when a prediction is disconfirmed by reliable evidence; null results are often as useful as positive results (something I see in my own AI research practice). However, success criteria are nevertheless ordinarily specified in advance with relative precision. In artistic practice, the aims and evaluative standards may themselves be transformed through making, so that failure relative to an original intention can acquire emergent artistic value [@andrzejewski2021failedart; @lefeuvre2010failure]. For instance, research comparing pharmaceutical development and music production suggest that, in both art and science, failure operates as a process through which practitioners reconsider methods and what is worth pursuing, but that this process differs in its details across domains [@otto2025failure]. Thought experiments may fail in an analogous productive sense: disagreement or “imaginative disharmony” can expose unstable assumptions, divergent interpretations or framing dependencies rather than simply yielding no philosophical result. On the other hand, they may fail because the depicted world is simply too far (psychologically speaking), too complex, or just too weird for the reader to form any reliable intuition, a risk that might apply to the Axiological Atlas [@molinari2022thought;@tuohimaa2025danger].